# Upstream refs this repo's CI builds its HIF dependencies from.
#
# These are passed straight to actions/checkout's `ref:`, which takes a branch
# name as readily as a commit SHA. They name `develop` deliberately: CI tracks
# the upstream development branch and there is nothing here to bump.
#
# This replaces per-commit pinning, which was accurate but had to be moved by
# hand on every upstream fix and rotted silently when nobody did - hif-muffin's
# pins had drifted 22 commits behind before anyone noticed. The trade was made
# knowingly, and both halves of it are real:
#
#   gained  - integration breakage surfaces the day it lands, and adopting an
#             upstream fix costs nothing.
#   lost    - a red check here no longer means "this diff broke something". It
#             can also mean an upstream commit landed. Before assuming your
#             change is at fault, look at the sibling repository's recent
#             history; the build log records which commit it checked out.
#
# One hazard this used to guard, worth knowing rather than rediscovering: a
# hif-backend at or past hif-backend#46 built against a hif-frontend before
# hif-frontend#46's tagging change demotes every Verilog process containing a
# wait to `initial`, silently stopping the design after its first pass. Pinning
# made that window explicit. Tracking develop does not, so if a cross-repo
# change needs both sides, land the upstream half first.
#
# Reproducibility now lives where it belongs rather than here: git history for
# any past build, and hif-regression's manifests/stable.yaml for the released
# toolchain baseline.
HIF_CORE_REF=develop
